<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Christian, D. (1999) A History of Russia, Mongolia and Central Asia. Blackwell.</li><li>Cross, S. H. and Sherbowitz-Wetzor, O. P. (trs. and eds.) (1930) The Russian Primary Chronicle, Laurentian Text. Cambridge, Massachusetts: The Mediaeval Academy of America.</li><li>Feldbrugge, F. J. M. (2009) Law in Medieval Russia. BRILL.</li><li>Feldbrugge, F. J. M. (2017) A History of Russian Law: From Ancient Times to the Council Code (Ulozhenie) of Tsar Aleksei Mikhailovich of 1649. BRILL.</li><li>Grekov, B. (ed.) (1940–1963) Pravda Russkaya. 3 vols. Moscow; Leningrad: publisher of the Academy of Sciences of the USSR.</li><li>Kaiser, D. H. (tr. and ed.) (1992) The Laws of Rus' - Tenth to Fifteenth Centuries. Salt Lake City: Charles Schlacks Publisher.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: Estimate, kept wide: the articles give no count: Kievan Rus' was &quot;sparsely populated compared to Western Europe&quot; but the largest European state by area; Novgorod had 10,000–15,000 people in 1000, Kiev 36,000–50,000 around 1200, and some 300 towns stood on the eve of the Mongol invasion.</p></section>
